  "body": "<p>Serath Vareth is pale, barefoot and cautious about the welcome she will receive. Her concern is personal: she says a follower of Cala has already attacked someone like her. Yet when she reaches a ceremony for Cala, she asks how she can help it continue.</p><p>That willingness becomes more costly in the First Forest. She takes a Delerium blow meant for Aionia, then gives her soul after the goddess falls. The choice belongs beside the person making it: a newcomer who wants protection herself and still keeps offering it to others.</p><h2 id=\"serath-asking-for-welcome\">Whether people like her may come in</h2><p>Walking with Moira toward a ceremony, Serath asks whether the place accepts people like her. She says she does not breathe or eat and describes an armored follower of Cala who attacked her. That is her account of an earlier encounter, not evidence that every follower will do the same.</p><p>Moira and Gregory give her company while she approaches. Serath stays close, asks what the ceremony means and keeps looking for the person who leads it. She wants permission to add light, then chants and imitates the gestures she sees. Being afraid of the welcome does not make her unwilling to participate.</p><p>When an exchange with a warrior might become a challenge, she steps back and watches anxiously. Her attention returns to the rite. At the end she thanks the elder for letting her help. The scene leaves her curious, hesitant and determined to find a way into the company around her.</p><h2 id=\"serath-questions-for-everafter\">A guest who asks about the missing</h2><p>On the visit to Mr. Everafter, Serath greets Djö and asks whether he is coming. She does not need the meal herself, but notices that her friend is hungry and asks for food for him. The practical care sits beside her own uncertainty about who and what they are meeting.</p><p>She looks for guardians, religious symbols and signs of the kinds of undead she knows. Some of her suspicions come from that expectation. She keeps watching the host and finally asks where the people who disappeared have been placed. It is a direct question, without a recovered answer that lets her solve their fate.</p><p>A later riddle draws another guess from her: perhaps the answer is a guest. She asks about leadership and organization, then tries to understand the movement and magic around the room before leaving by boat. The visit shows her asking for explanations rather than accepting an impressive setting as its own reassurance.</p><h2 id=\"serath-the-cost-she-chooses\">The hurt she will not walk past</h2><p>The golden trail in the First Forest gives Serath another person to worry about. She wants to find the wounded celestial, helps Modeli tend Arethusa and is herself brought back to her feet when distorted magic makes her collapse. She is still holding Modeli’s hand as they approach Aionia.</p><p>When Aleister throws Delerium, Serath intercepts it with her chest. She remains in front of Aionia, then stays to defend her against the mist. The explanation she gives is the suffering already before her: the goddess is bleeding for others, and Serath cannot ignore it.</p><p>After Aionia falls, Serath places the life-tether ankh on her and offers herself. The first attempt fails, but she stays and tries again. Thorn and Modeli also offer themselves; the narrator ultimately shows Serath’s soul entering Aionia and her body falling. The goddess lives, then receives the Great Stag’s strength. <a href=\"#seraths-sacrifice\">Read the complete quest →</a></p><h2 id=\"serath-the-people-left\">The people who have to remember</h2><p>Aionia cradles Serath’s body and weeps. She asks Modeli to remember her, then restores Aleister and returns to battle. Modeli promises remembrance, blames himself and carries Serath toward Pristinia. Thorn’s prayer asks for her name to remain in flowing water, wind and the care people give one another.</p><p>Those responses are different ways of bearing the same loss. The next day Pristinia is full of questions. At the leadership tests Modeli still hopes for a revival; in the later journey into The Before, he still thinks of her as dead. These later scenes preserve grief and hope, without supplying a witnessed return for her.</p><p>Her history does not end with a claim that she had always wanted to die. The earlier Serath wanted safety, company, explanations and a place to offer help. The sacrifice shows what she finally chose for someone she saw suffering. <a href=\"#modeli\">Modeli</a> · <a href=\"#thorn\">Thorn</a> · <a href=\"#aionia\">Aionia</a></p>",
